# === CURRENT DIRECTION (decode, graph-ON) — pinned 2026-06-27, START HERE ===

Authoritative profile: `utilities/glm5_fp4_decode_prof_0626.csv` (GLM-5 FP4 DECODE,
in1024/out1024/conc4, graph-ON, layer-by-layer MI355X mxfp4 vs B200 nvfp4, per-leaf
kernels + call sites). SUPERSEDES the old c=8 gap table (deleted).

Finding: in a single decode step MI355X total kernel duration is only ~7% higher than
B200 (Σdur 17,636 vs 16,474 us = 93%). The difference is OVERLAP: B200 uses multiple
streams to overlap kernels and cut wall-clock; MI355X runs them essentially serially.
Net wall-clock per decode step at CONC=4: **MI355X 18.1 ms vs B200 12.3 ms** (B200
~32% faster = 68%; overlap 1.43x vs 1.00x).

DECISION — multi-stream is OFF the table. Tried on the FP8 model: stream bubbles + sync
overhead made performance WORSE. Do NOT propose multi-stream / overlap scheduling again.

Plans (attack the ~93% raw kernel work, NOT overlap):

1. **GEMM / MoE tuning (~40% of the decode step)** — these kernels currently run
   UNTUNED (server log: "not found tuned config … using torch solution:0" and
   "[fused_moe] using 2stage default"). Two separate tuning paths:
   - bf16 dense GEMM (`hgemm_bf16`, Cijk/hipBLASLt) → aiter `gemm_tuner` →
     `bf16_tuned_gemm.csv`.
   - fp4 fused-MoE (`mfma_moe1` / `mfma_moe2`) → aiter `fmoe_2stages` / `tune.py` →
     `tuned_fmoe.csv`.
   (The DECODE kernels fall back to torch/default tiles, so the target is
   tuned-vs-untuned, not re-tuning an already-tuned tile. Gate on graphs-on e2e A/B.)

2. **Indexer kernel fusion** — the DSA indexer (inside DeepseekV2AttentionMLA) takes
   137 of 230 us in one layer and issues many tiny kernels (~4 us each: qk_rmsnorm,
   rope+cache, k_quant, hadamard, topk, …). Fusing them reduces per-kernel overhead and
   improves GPU utilization for these under-occupied launches.

