## DECODE ATTENTION main_kernel — PMC diagnosis (2026-06-29) — NEW LEVER, not yet A/B'd

The decode attention `main_kernel` is NOT aiter asm — it is the TileLang two-stage
split-KV flash-decode in `python/sglang/srt/layers/attention/dsa/tilelang_kernel.py`:
`tilelang_sparse_fwd` -> `sparse_mla_fwd_decode_partial_fp8` (partial) + `_combine`.
This is IN-REPO and editable (vs the vendor GEMMs). Driver:
`utilities/bench_tilelang_sparse_decode.py` (symbolic seq_len -> sweep bs in 1 process;
PRE-WARM compile cache WITHOUT rocprof first, then rocprof reuses cache — a fresh
JIT-under-rocprof recompile hangs ~10min at 197% CPU / 0% GPU).

rocprofv3 single-pass PMC (GPU4, e4m3fn, topk=2048, 16 heads/tp4), partial kernel
(VGPR=56, SGPR=32, LDS reported 0, WG=256), `inner_iter=1` regime:

| bs | blocks | mean_us | Occ% | MfmaUtil% | VALUBusy% |
|----|--------|---------|------|-----------|-----------|
|  1 |     32 |  9.12   | 0.69 | 0.17      | 1.2       |
|  2 |     64 |  9.08   | 1.34 | 0.34      | 2.4       |
|  4 |    128 |  9.40   | 2.68 | 0.67      | 4.8       |
|  8 |    256 | 10.10   | 5.30 | 1.30      | 9.3       |
| 16 |    512 | 13.16   | 11.05| 2.33      | 16.7      |

bs>=32 the `_pick_inner_iter` heuristic raises inner_iter (32->2,64->4,128->8), keeping
~512 blocks; per-call drops to 4.5-5.8us, Occ still <=16%, MfmaUtil <=5% (best case).

DIAGNOSIS: **latency-bound, never compute-bound.** (1) Per-call time is ~FLAT (~9us)
bs1->bs8 while work grows 8x — extra blocks run "for free" on idle CUs. (2) MfmaUtil <5%
and VALUBusy <17% in EVERY regime — ALU/MFMA idle. (3) Occupancy 0.7-16% — at <16% you
physically cannot be HBM-bandwidth-bound (too few in-flight requests), so it is
LATENCY-bound (sparse-KV gather latency) with too few waves to hide it. NOT occupancy-
resource-limited (VGPR=56/LDS low admit many waves) — it is GRID-STARVED at low conc:
gfx95 block_I=64 caps n_groups=topk/64=32, so grid=bs*32; the 256-CU MI355X wants ~512
blocks (block_per_cu=2), reached only at bs>=16. (MI355X 256 CUs vs B200 132 SMs => harder
to fill at low batch — structural.)

PROPOSED LEVER (passes the kernel-opt gate; still must be A/B'd): give the kernel more
concurrent waves to hide gather latency at low conc — finer KV-split (smaller block_I =>
more n_groups => more blocks) when grid < ~2*CU, leaving the saturated high-conc regime
(bs>=32, already splitting less) unchanged. Gate logic: AMD decode runs SERIALLY
(overlap 1.00x per pinned finding) so main_kernel time is ADDITIVE to wall-clock (unlike
the overlapped fused-rope dead-end), and attention is a RAW gap (10,329 vs 8,352us), not
overlap. Predicted: lower attention time at low/mid conc; possible neutral at high conc.
PROOF REQUIRED: graphs-on e2e A/B (`e2e_glm5.sh`), not isolated kernel time.

# GLM-5.1-MXFP4 prefill optimization log (gfx950 / DSA path)

Read this before re-profiling or proposing prefill optimizations for GLM5 (or
any DSA / DeepSeek-sparse-attention model) on gfx950. It records what already
shipped and — more importantly — what has already been tried and rejected, so we
do not go in circles.

## STOP — kernel-opt gate (MANDATORY before touching ANY kernel)

This is a hard gate, not advice. It exists because "knowing the rule" has failed
repeatedly: the cheap measurement (an isolated kernel time) front-runs the true
one (graphs-on e2e), hands you a seductive multiplier, and you ship a zero.

Before implementing ANY kernel optimization, write down and answer IN ADVANCE:

1. **What is the phase BOUND on?** compute / memory-bandwidth / collective(comm)
   / launch-latency. (Decode here is collective+latency-bound; prefill is comm +
   attention.)
2. **Is this kernel on the UN-OVERLAPPED critical path of that binding resource?**
   Under HIP graphs (decode) — and even eager prefill — independent kernels
   overlap across queues, so **a kernel's TRACE TIME is NOT its marginal
   step-time cost.** A kernel that overlaps a neighbor, or sits behind the
   binding constraint, has marginal cost ~0 no matter how big its trace time.
3. **Predicted graphs-on e2e delta, with reasoning.** If the kernel is overlapped
   or behind the binding constraint → predicted delta ~0 → **DO NOT implement.**

Rules that follow from the gate:
- **Trace times are HYPOTHESES, never "potential e2e win"** — this includes
  rocprof graphs-off traces, torch-profiler traces, AND the B200 per-kernel gap
  table. Ranking kernels by trace-time delta assumes times are additive /
  on-critical-path; under overlap they are not.
- **rocprof forces graphs-off** (segfaults graphs-on), so the only kernel number
  you can cheaply get is the serial one — which over-attributes time to
  overlapped kernels. Treat its multiplier as a hypothesis; never report it as a
  win.
- **The ONLY result is a graphs-on e2e A/B delta** (`e2e_glm5.sh`, read TTFT for
  prefill + ITL/throughput for decode).

Worked example (why this gate exists): the indexer **fused-rope** swap was
lossless and **2.56x on the isolated graphs-off kernel** (aiter 9.0us -> sgl
3.5us) — and **graphs-on e2e-NEUTRAL** (ITL -0.5..-1.3% noise, TTFT +2-3% noise,
tput flat), so it was removed. Root cause: the rope is overlapped compute sitting
behind the collective-bound decode critical path, so its marginal cost is ~0. The
"~5% slice" framing was itself wrong — the binding-constraint contribution was ~0,
not 5%. Same root cause as MoE-tuning and the split-KV decode kernel: all three
were graphs-off wins on overlapped / non-critical-path work.

## Methodology — read this FIRST (hard-won; ignore at your peril)

The dominant failure mode this whole effort: **hunting the biggest kernel and
blindly making it "faster" / tuning it.** Track record on this config — several
narrow kernel-level fixes (K-fusion, decode split-KV kernel, AllReduce fusion,
query hadamard fusion) all either regressed e2e, were inert, or were marginal
(~1%). The ONE big win (EAGLE spec decoding,
2.6-3.3x ITL) was **structural**, not a kernel swap. Why the kernel-hunt keeps
failing here:

1. **This system is not compute-bound.** Decode is collective/latency-bound
   (per-layer TP all-reduce on the critical path); prefill is comm + attention.
   Speeding up a compute kernel that's behind a CUDA graph (which already hides
   its launch cost) and is 10% of the time → ~0 e2e. A faster kernel only helps a
   compute-bound phase.
2. **Production has dynamic shapes; static gates miss.** Continuous batching +
   chunked prefill produce a *distribution* of M/shapes. A kernel tuned/gated to
   one shape (M=8192, 16 heads, …) can fail to be hit at runtime, so an isolated
   win need not translate — always confirm with a graphs-on e2e A/B.
3. **Isolated/graphs-off microbench ≠ graphs-on production.** Three "wins"
   inverted under the real regime.

**The corrected method:**
- **Bottleneck-first, not hot-kernel-first.** Identify what the system is *bound
  on*, then pick a lever that attacks THAT. Prefer **structural levers** that
  change the amount/pattern of work — spec decoding (fewer forward steps →
  fewer collectives/token), quantization (less traffic), parallelism strategy
  (DP-attn / EP / two-batch-overlap), caching, *removing* redundant work
  (#28757) — over kernel micro-opts. They translate and aren't shape-brittle.
- **Narrow kernel opt is justified ONLY when the phase is genuinely
  compute-bound AND the shape is production-stable.** That niche is why #28975
  (prefill attention, large-M, compute-bound, fixed 16-head shape) is the one
  kernel opt that worked — and it is rare and mostly already taken by the vendor.
- **When a kernel IS the right target, do not blindly rewrite/tune it — profile
  the kernel itself with rocprof hardware counters to find what it actually
  needs.** Is it VGPR-spilling (occupancy-limited)? Is VALU mostly idle
  (memory/latency-bound, not ALU-bound)? Low MFMA utilization (dependency
  stalls)? LDS-bound? Bad wave occupancy? The fix follows the diagnosis
  (e.g. spilling → reduce live regs / smaller tile; idle VALU → it's
  memory-bound, a faster ALU tile won't help; dep-stall → restructure the
  pipeline). Use `utilities/rocprof_pmc_analyze.py` for the counter pass.
- **Validate only with graphs-on e2e A/B** (`utilities/e2e_glm5.sh`). Kill the
  isolated-kernel-time habit entirely.

